C MP-reorder
"Message passing with two INDEPENDENT writes on the writer and two independent
 reads on the reader (no syntactic dependency between the pairs). This is the
 'reordering of independent memory accesses' shape. OHMM permits the
 non-message-passing outcome r1=1 /\ r2=0: its abstract machine has a global
 event buffer in which independent (non-dependent) events may be reordered
 before they propagate, so the store to y can surface before the store to x.
 LKMM, whose ordering is assembled from explicit Linux barriers, has NO such
 reordering for these plain READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE accesses across smp_wmb()/
 smp_rmb(): with the barriers present it FORBIDS the outcome, and the two models
 also disagree on which trace-preserving transformations are sound (Table 3 of
 the survey: LKMM and OHMM have different transformation profiles). Conversely
 LKMM expresses RCU and smp_* barriers OHMM cannot, so neither subsumes the
 other. Neither verdict is machine-run here: OHMM has no herd7/cat model, and
 the plain-access reordering is OHMM-specific, not an LKMM execution. Verdicts
 are CITED from the literature. See README."
{ int x=0; int y=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  atomic_store_explicit(x,1,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(y,1,memory_order_relaxed);
}
P1 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r1 = atomic_load_explicit(y,memory_order_relaxed);
  int r2 = atomic_load_explicit(x,memory_order_relaxed);
}
exists (1:r1=1 /\ 1:r2=0)
